  "metadata": {
    "title": "Anthropic Hits $47 Billion Run-Rate as AI Price War Fears Rise",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "This article first appeared on GuruFocus.\nOpenAI and Anthropic may be entering a more difficult stretch as corporate customers grow more sensitive to the rising cost of AI tools. OpenAI CEO Sam Altman has acknowledged that costs have become a huge issue for customers, while reportedly weighing a major price cut as Anthropic continues gaining ground in the enterprise market. The shift comes as companies that once raced to adopt AI coding and reasoning tools are now reviewing budgets, limiting usage, and asking whether the spending is producing a clear return.\nAnthropic's momentum has been one of the biggest drivers behind its valuation. The company's revenue run-rate has reached $47 billion, roughly five times higher than six months ago, while its valuation stands at $965 billion, compared with OpenAI's $852 billion. Its Claude Code tool has helped fuel strong enterprise demand, but concerns are building that corporate AI spending could slow if customers move toward cheaper models, usage limits, or good enough alternatives.\nThe pressure could also ripple across the wider AI infrastructure trade. Amazon (NASDAQ:AMZN), Microsoft (NASDAQ:MSFT), Oracle (NYSE:ORCL), Alphabet (NASDAQ:GOOGL), and other hyperscalers have been spending heavily on data centers and compute capacity tied to demand from major AI labs. Oracle shares fell in pre-market trading Thursday amid concerns over its data-center investments, while Anthropic has signed a $1.25 billion-a-month processing capacity deal with Elon Musk's SpaceX and major compute agreements with Alphabet and Amazon. If pricing pressure intensifies while computing costs remain high, investors may begin questioning whether today's AI revenue surge can translate into sustained profitability.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "This article first appeared on GuruFocus.",
      "OpenAI and Anthropic may be entering a more difficult stretch as corporate customers grow more sensitive to the rising cost of AI tools. OpenAI CEO Sam Altman has acknowledged that costs have become a huge issue for customers, while reportedly weighing a major price cut as Anthropic continues gaining ground in the enterprise market. The shift comes as companies that once raced to adopt AI coding and reasoning tools are now reviewing budgets, limiting usage, and asking whether the spending is producing a clear return.",
      "Anthropic's momentum has been one of the biggest drivers behind its valuation. The company's revenue run-rate has reached $47 billion, roughly five times higher than six months ago, while its valuation stands at $965 billion, compared with OpenAI's $852 billion. Its Claude Code tool has helped fuel strong enterprise demand, but concerns are building that corporate AI spending could slow if customers move toward cheaper models, usage limits, or good enough alternatives.",
      "The pressure could also ripple across the wider AI infrastructure trade. Amazon (NASDAQ:AMZN), Microsoft (NASDAQ:MSFT), Oracle (NYSE:ORCL), Alphabet (NASDAQ:GOOGL), and other hyperscalers have been spending heavily on data centers and compute capacity tied to demand from major AI labs. Oracle shares fell in pre-market trading Thursday amid concerns over its data-center investments, while Anthropic has signed a $1.25 billion-a-month processing capacity deal with Elon Musk's SpaceX and major compute agreements with Alphabet and Amazon. If pricing pressure intensifies while computing costs remain high, investors may begin questioning whether today's AI revenue surge can translate into sustained profitability."
    ],
